<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tancred, Prince of Galilee</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1075–1112</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Tancred of Hauteville</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tancred was an Italo-Norman leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a> and a nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemond-i-of-antioch">Bohemond of Taranto</a>. After the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 1099 he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberias">Tiberias</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a> and styled himself prince there. He later ruled the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> as regent, expanding it at the expense of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tancred-prince-of-galilee.jpg" alt="Tancred, Prince of Galilee" width="320" height="162" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tankreds_coin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tancred was most likely born in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> to Odo, called the Good Marquis, and Emma of Hauteville, a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-guiscard">Robert Guiscard</a>. Most medieval authors, including William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>, make him a nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemond-i-of-antioch">Bohemond I of Antioch</a> through his mother, though Ralph of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caen">Caen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orderic-vitalis">Orderic Vitalis</a> mistook Emma for Guiscard's sister. His link to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hauteville">House of Hauteville</a> came only through Emma. Some modern scholars place his father's family among the Aleramids, a ruling house of northern Italy.</p><p>In 1096 he joined Bohemond on the First Crusade. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> he refused to swear the oath to Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-i-komnenos">Alexios I Komnenos</a> that the other leaders took, and the surrender of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> to Alexios's army deepened his distrust of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>. In 1097 he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarsus">Tarsus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adana">Adana</a> and Mopsuestia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a>. He claimed, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaston-iv-of-bearn">Gaston IV of Béarn</a>, to have been the first crusader to enter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> on 15 July 1099. During the sack he gave his banner to citizens sheltering on the roof of the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>, but other crusaders massacred them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a> was established, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-of-bouillon">Godfrey of Bouillon</a> enfeoffed Tancred with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberias">Tiberias</a>, and he held the nucleus of the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a>. When Bohemond was captured at the Battle of Melitene in August 1100, Tancred took over the regency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> by March 1101. On his coinage he called himself servant of God or grand emir, not prince. With Genoese help he took Lattakiah in April or May 1103 after a siege of 18 months, and in 1105 he defeated Ridwan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> near Artah.</p><p>After the crusaders' defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a> in 1104, Tancred also governed Edessa, but in 1108 he had to give it back to Baldwin of Le Bourcq. Bohemond left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and Tancred ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> as he wished. Between 1109 and 1111 he conquered the whole of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a>, and in 1110 he brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krak-des-chevaliers">Krak des Chevaliers</a> under his control. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-i-of-jerusalem">Baldwin I of Jerusalem</a> restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a> to him in 1109 on condition that he became the king's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Tancred remained regent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> for Bohemond II until his death in 1112 during a typhoid epidemic. He had married Cecile of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and died childless, and was buried in the porch of the cathedral of St Peter at Antioch. His first biography, the Gesta Tancredi, was written about 1120 by Ralph of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caen">Caen</a>, who served under him. It depicts a pious but violent and opportunistic warrior who was troubled by the sinfulness of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knighthood">knightly</a> profession. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torquato-tasso">Torquato Tasso</a> made him an epic hero in Jerusalem Delivered in 1581, and later works by Monteverdi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a> and Rossini drew on his story.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tancred of Galilee” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Haifa was taken in August 1100 by <b>Tancred</b> with the help of a Venetian fleet, after a stubborn defence in which its Jewish and Muslim inhabitants fought together.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-haifa">Lordship of Haifa</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Tancred</b>, the Norman crusader, took Tiberias and the surrounding region soon after the capture of Jerusalem in 1099 and styled himself Prince of Galilee.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-galilee">Principality of Galilee</a></li><li><blockquote>Ridwan lost territory to the Principality of Antioch and paid tribute to <b>Tancred</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/seljuk-emirate-of-aleppo">Seljuk Emirate of Aleppo</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.2,36.15&amp;year=1099" aria-label="Antioch on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5460 857 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Tancred, Prince of Galilee"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5910 1127h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5910 1127h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=36.2,36.15&amp;year=1099">What was here in 1099? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
